#Q3: SYSTEM HARDENING TASKS

##Definition
System hardening is the process of reducing a system's attack surface by disabling unnecessary features, enforcing access controls, and applying security configurations.

##Steps and Commands

(a) Block Ethernet Interface
```bash
Linux
sudo ifconfig eth0 down

Verify
ip addr show eth0
State should show DOWN

Windows (Admin CMD)
netsh interface set interface "Ethernet" disable
```

(b) Configure Firewall
```bash
Linux: Allow port 80 and 443, block all other inbound
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A INPUT -j DROP

Verify rules are in place
sudo iptables -L -n

Windows (Admin CMD)
netsh advfirewall set allprofiles firewallpolicy blockinbound,allowoutbound
netsh advfirewall firewall add rule name="Allow-HTTP" dir=in action=allow protocol=tcp localport=80
netsh advfirewall firewall add rule name="Allow-HTTPS" dir=in action=allow protocol=tcp localport=443
```

(c) Disable USB Storage
```bash
Linux
echo 'blacklist usb-storage' | sudo tee /etc/modprobe.d/usb-block.conf
sudo modprobe -r usb-storage

Verify
lsmod | grep usb_storage
Should return nothing if disabled

Windows (Registry - Admin CMD)
reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\USBSTOR" /v Start /t REG_DWORD /d 4 /f
4 = Disabled. 3 = Enabled (to re-enable, set back to 3).
```

(d) Disable CD-ROM
```bash
Linux: blacklist the optical drive kernel module
echo 'blacklist cdrom' | sudo tee /etc/modprobe.d/cdrom-block.conf
echo 'blacklist sr_mod' | sudo tee -a /etc/modprobe.d/cdrom-block.conf
sudo modprobe -r cdrom sr_mod

Verify
lsmod | grep cdrom
Should return nothing

Windows: disable via Device Manager
Device Manager > DVD/CD-ROM drives > right-click > Disable device
OR via registry:
reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\cdrom" /v Start /t REG_DWORD /d 4 /f
```

(e) Enable Login Authorisation (Password Policy + Lockout)
```bash
Linux: Set 90-day password expiry
sudo passwd -x 90 username
sudo passwd -n 7 username     minimum days before user can change again
sudo passwd -w 14 username    warn user 14 days before expiry

Account lockout after 5 failed attempts (edit PAM config)
sudo nano /etc/pam.d/common-auth
Add this line at the top of the file:
auth required pam_tally2.so onerr=fail deny=5 unlock_time=300

Windows (Admin CMD)
net accounts /maxpwage:90 /minpwlen:8 /lockoutthreshold:5 /lockoutduration:5

Windows: enable via secpol.msc
Security Settings > Account Policies > Password Policy
Security Settings > Account Policies > Account Lockout Policy
```

##Final Report (the Jun 2025 question explicitly asks for this)

Write this as a summary table after completing all tasks:

| | Task | Action Taken | Tool/Command Used | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Block Ethernet | Disabled eth0 / Ethernet adapter | ifconfig / netsh | Done |
| 2 | Configure Firewall | Allow 80, 443 inbound; block all else | iptables / netsh advfirewall | Done |
| 3 | Disable USB | Blacklisted usb-storage module | modprobe / registry | Done |
| 4 | Disable CD-ROM | Blacklisted cdrom and sr_mod | modprobe / registry | Done |
| 5 | Login authorisation | 90-day expiry, lockout after 5 fails | passwd / net accounts | Done |

Report conclusion line to write: "All five hardening tasks have been applied. The system's attack surface has been reduced by eliminating external storage vectors, restricting network access to defined ports only, and enforcing authentication controls."

##Key Concepts
- Least privilege: Users get only the access they need. Nothing more.
- Attack surface reduction: Disabling unused services, ports, and interfaces removes entry points for attackers.
- CIS Benchmarks: Published hardening guides for every major OS. Used as the industry baseline for what to disable and what to enforce.
- Blacklisting a kernel module: Prevents Linux from loading a driver at boot. The hardware is physically present but the OS cannot communicate with it.

##Viva
- Q: Why disable USB storage specifically? A: USB devices are a common vector for data exfiltration and malware introduction (BadUSB attacks). Disabling the driver blocks these even if the port is physically accessible.
- Q: Why blacklist cdrom separately from USB? A: They use different kernel modules. Disabling USB storage does not affect optical drives because they are controlled by the cdrom/sr_mod module.
- Q: What is the risk of a default-deny firewall rule without testing? A: Legitimate services can be blocked. Always test in a staging environment before applying to production.

